  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nHot, dry, and unseasonably warm conditions dominate across \neastern Idaho as strong upper-level ridging amplifies over the \nIntermountain West. Valley temperatures have already scaled into\nthe lower 90s early this afternoon and remain on track to top \nout in the mid-to-upper 90s during peak diurnal heating. \nConsequently, Heat Advisories remain active for lower-elevation \nzones through this evening. H5 heights will continue to rise \ninto Friday, nudging afternoon maximum temperatures up by an \nadditional 1 to 3 degrees over today's readings. An expanded \nHeat Advisory will take effect Friday to account for this \nsecondary surge in thermal stress. While satellite imagery \ncurrently reveals mostly sunny skies, high-resolution \nconvective-allowing models (CAMs) continue to show a low-end \n(~15%) chance for an isolated shower or thunderstorm across \nsouthern zones late this afternoon and evening.\n\nA synoptic transition begins tonight into Friday as an upper- \nlevel low-pressure system moves inland over the Pacific \nNorthwest. This feature will back regional flow to the \nsouthwest, opening a mid-to-upper level moisture channel into \nIdaho. Cloud cover will steadily increase overnight, with \nisolated to scattered shower and thunderstorm chances expanding \nacross the forecast area through Friday afternoon.\n\nSaturday continues to stand out as the height of convective \ncoverage across the region. The combination of advancing \nshortwave dynamics, upper-level divergence, and elevated \nprecipitable water (PWAT) values will support widespread showers\nand embedded thunderstorms. As the primary upper low tracks \nnortheastward toward the Canadian Prairies on Sunday, sufficient\nmid-level lift and moisture will linger across the northern and\neastern highlands to round out the weekend with continued \nscattered precipitation chances.\n\nLong-range guidance indicates that the broad Four Corners high-\npressure ridge will attempt to re-amplify across the Great Basin\nheading into the new workweek. However, the ridge axis does not\nappear strong enough to completely cap the atmosphere. Lingering\nboundary-layer moisture will combine with daily diurnal heating\nto maintain isolated shower and thunderstorm chances through at\nleast the first half of next week. Afternoon temperatures will\nmoderate slightly behind the weekend trough but will hold near\n90 degrees across valley floors \u2014 remaining a few degrees above\nseasonal climatological norms.",
